{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-08-perfect-square-surds-nested-roots",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Perfect-Square Surds and Nested Roots",
    "description": "Turn nested square roots into simple surd sums and differences by spotting hidden perfect squares.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach one visual template repeatedly: p+q±2√(pq) is (√p±√q)^2. Every example identifies the two numbers p and q, squares the proposed simplification as a check, and only then moves to a matching practice question.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach the hidden-square pattern (√p±√q)^2=p+q±2√(pq). Begin by matching a constant sum and the product under the inner square root, then simplify positive square roots such as √(5+2√6), √(8−2√15), and chapter-style expressions where two conjugate nested roots are added or subtracted. Include cases with coefficients outside the radical and the reverse operation of squaring a sum of surds. Learners should check a proposed simplification by squaring it mentally; this is the fastest way to catch a wrong sign or wrong pair."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A nested radical such as √(5+2√6) looks complicated only until you recognise a square hiding inside it.\n\nThe goal is not to expand everything. It is to find two numbers whose sum and product create the expression.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Start with 3+2√2.\n\nCan it be the square of √2+1?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "(√2+1)^2=2+1+2√2=3+2√2.\n\nSo √(3+2√2)=√2+1.\n\nThe hidden pair is p=2 and q=1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "√(6+2√5) = ?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "√5+1"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "√5−1"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "5+1"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "√6+√5"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
        "mode": "INLINE",
        "inlineData": {
